Date: Tue, 27 Apr 93 19:43:02 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Earth Day

KMOSTA01 writes:

> The statistical data of how many environmentalists are this or that
> kind of leftists is not as important, in my opinion, as the very fact
> that environmentalist movement is rooted in the concept of
> transspecies responsibility

Mmph.  There is some of that (probably a lot).  Still, though,
let me explain.

I am not a "leftist" (if I understand the term -- that centralized
resource allocation is the solution to social problems).  I am a
minarchist who wishes I was an anarchist and looks forward to
the (perhaps technological) solution to the remaining problems
with pure anarchy (see MoF for the most important).  Despite that,
I'm (marginally) less nauseated at a President Clinton than more
of a President Bush (and I voted for Marrou).  Mostly, it doesn't
seem to matter much.  The much-vaunted "Reagan Revolution" seemed
to mostly involve conflicts between Executive and Legislative that
led to much larger government financed by borrowed money.  I detected
no appreciable increase in personal freedom.  Most of my in-the-flesh
friends are Democrats -- partly because the city in which I live
is VERY liberal (the mayor tried to get a city-wide handgun ban
passed); partly because the Republicans I know seem somehow less
fun-loving.  Big deal.

I believe that U.S. government, if we can prevent it from insidiously
taking more of our freedom away (and slight up-and-down blips in tax
rates hardly matter much), technology will provide the liberating
force in the next century.  That is why I focus my study and efforts
along those lines.  Political squabbling seems rather like shouting
at a freight train.  There are exceptions -- despite my thoughts
about the Clipper proposal (which I still think are more or less
accurate), Perry said in a different forum something like "over-
reaction is the best way we preserve our rights", a statement with
which I agree completely.

Anyway, the environment.  I am very concerned about the data about
the environment that is constantly being collected and analyzed.
Both sides (the radical "environmentalists" and the reactionary
corporates) point to their favorite studies to support their
ideological position.  As neither, I remain concerned.  The
stakes are quite high.  I do not want to have to devote technological
development to some crisis in thirty years if a thinning ozone
layer leads to some awful plague among the plankton or if there
does turn out to be something to the concern over the decimation
of CO2-absorbing organisms in the rain forests.  I believe that
there will be technological means to stop and reverse any such
damages; the question is when they will arrive.

For this reason (and one other, see below), I consider myself
an "environmentalist".  However, I cringe because I do not
recognize the legitimacy of the federal government's role
in interpreting the data and issuing decrees based on largely
political and self-serving motives.  I am rather more sympathetic
to regulation at the lowest levels of government; the small-town
or community can ALMOST be thought of as an "intentional" group
and if all regulation was done at such a level, like-minded people
could easily find places where the rules were to their liking.

The other reason that I am a (self-described) environmentalist
is that I truly enjoy outdoor activity away from people and
noise.  Every year I go off for weeks to the stunning and
available wild places, trying to be conscientious to be
low-impact.  Although I do not recognize the right of large-scale
regulatory bodies to make sure that I retain that joyous
part of my life, I would be devastated if all of that land
were overrun with burger kings and strip malls.

A while ago, somebody posted a wonderful slogan that went
something like "Earth:  Ours by right of conquest to do
with as we will."  Since then, the chorus of a proto folk tune
called "right of conquest" has been running through my head...
perhaps I'll finish it and play it for some of you someday.
But though I agree completely with the sentiment, I don't
think it's at all at odds with "environmentalism"... for
my house and yard, it's called "upkeep".  Yes, the Earth is
ours... it only seems sensible to be a bit careful what we
do with it.

> It's the idea that counts. It reads: "We want to kill you. If we can't
> kill you, we'll make we control your every move and make your life
> miserable."

Oh, please.  You should get out more.

derek

