Perry Metzger wrote:
 
  her belief in absolute moral systems and the like is flawed

I think it's important to point out that Rand formed her whole
philosophy in reaction to monstrous evils that are no longer prominent
on the world intellectual scene. She had a very extreme reaction, but
the evils she was attempting to destroy were at least equally extreme.

I agree with your above statement. But the tiresome scenes in ATLAS
SHRUGGED in which the villains repeatedly ask "Who are you to know?
Why should you trust your own judgement? How can anyone know whether
anything is real?" reflect real arguments that were used repeatedly in
the Thirties to undermine classical liberal criticism of the Stalin,
Hitler, and Roosevelt regimes. The use of "impractical" as a
pejorative description of classical liberal "idealism" was also
widespread. Rand perceived, correctly in my view, that to counter the
insidiousness of these ideas, she needed to place classical liberal
ideas on an extremely firm epistemological basis. She was not a good
enough epistemologist to do this successfully, but she was one of the
first to attempt it, at least in a way that got any popular exposure
at all. Von Mises' praxeology, while still imperfect, was much better,
but has only recently achieved even the smallest amount of popular
exposure.

The vicious 1930's arguments Rand was attempting to destroy have since
died a natural death, and this makes her work look less important to
us now than it really was in a historical context. These arguments
were still running around in the 1960's, and I don't know how much or
how little effect ATLAS SHRUGGED had in laying them to rest. But I am
thankful that Rand tried.

